{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2349 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "599245f6c2d83e2fb954c84d5f53cb9e57574d92a4626bc47c190556ca032ee3" :edb/db-before-t 2348 :edb/db-after-t 2349 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535661 50 #inst "2026-10-09T04:37:44.741Z" 13194139535661 true] [17592186119501 1100 "Consider a curve $\\mathbf r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$. Find the radius of curvature of $\\mathbf r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that its unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(s)$ is given by\n$T(s)|=-\\sin (\\frac{s}{3})i + \\cos (\\frac{s}{3})j$." 13194139535661 false] [17592186119501 1100 "Consider a curve $\\mathbf r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $\\mathbf r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that  its  unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535661 true] [17592186119501 1145 "Since the curve $\\mathbf{r}(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(s) = \\dfrac{1}{\\kappa(s)} = \\dfrac{1}{\\|\\mathbf T'(s)\\|}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $\\mathbf T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{′}(s) &= \\frac{d}{ds}(-\\sin (\\frac{s}{3}))i + \\frac{d}{ds}(\\cos (\\frac{s}{3}))j \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{3}\\cos (\\frac{s}{3})i - \\frac{1}{3}\\sin (\\frac{s}{3})j \\\\\n‖T^{′}(s)‖ &= \\sqrt{(-\\frac{1}{3}\\cos (\\frac{s}{3}))^{2} + (-\\frac{1}{3}\\sin (\\frac{s}{3}))^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}(\\cos^{2} (\\frac{s}{3}) + \\sin^{2} (\\frac{s}{3}))} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s) = \\dfrac{1}{\\|\\mathbf T'(s)\\|} = \\dfrac{1}{\\:\\dfrac{1}{3}\\:} = 3\\,\n$$" 13194139535661 false] [17592186119501 1145 "Since the curve $\\mathbf{r}(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s,$ we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(s) = \\dfrac{1}{\\kappa(s)} = \\dfrac{1}{\\|\\mathbf T'(s)\\|}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $\\mathbf T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))^{2}+(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s) = \\dfrac{1}{\\|\\mathbf T'(s)\\|} = \\dfrac{1}{\\:\\dfrac{1}{3}\\:} = 3\\,.\n$$" 13194139535661 true] [17592186119502 1100 "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at the point $\\mathbf{r}(0)$, if the curve and the derivative $\\mathbf{T}'(t)$ of the corresponding unit tangent vector are given by\n\n$\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t, \\:\\dfrac{t^2}{2}\\right\\rangle, \\qquad \\mathbf T'(t)= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{(t^2+1)^3}}\\left\\langle -t, \\: 1\\right\\rangle \\,$." 13194139535661 false] [17592186119502 1100 "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at the point $\\mathbf{r}(0),$ if the curve and the derivative $\\mathbf{T}'(t)$ of the corresponding unit tangent vector are given by\n\n \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t, \\:\\dfrac{ t^2}{2}\\right\\rangle, \\qquad \\mathbf T'(t)= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{(t^2+1)^3}}\\left\\langle -t, \\:  1\\right\\rangle \\,.\n$$" 13194139535661 true] [17592186119502 1145 "Since we are given both the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ and the derivative $\\mathbf T'(t)$ of the unit tangent, we will use the following formula to compute the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\mathbf \\kappa(t)} = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|}{\\left\\| \\mathbf T'(t) \\right\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$, $\\mathbf r'(0)$, and $\\|\\mathbf r'(0)\\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨1, t⟩ \\\\\nr^{′}(0) &= ⟨1, 0⟩ \\\\\n‖r^{′}(0)‖ &= \\sqrt{1^{2} + 0^{2}} = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate $\\mathbf T'(0)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{′}(0) &= ⟨ - (0)/(\\sqrt{(1 + 0^{2})^{3}}), (1)/(\\sqrt{(1 + 0^{2})^{3}})⟩ = ⟨0, 1⟩ \\\\\n‖T^{′}(0)‖ &= \\sqrt{0^{2} + 1^{2}} = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(0) &= (‖r^{′}(0)‖)/(‖T^{′}(0)‖) = \\frac{1}{1} = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t = 0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(0) &= (T^{′}(0))/(‖T^{′}(0)‖) = ⟨0, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(0) + R(0)N(0) &=  \\\\\n⟨0, 0⟩ + 1 \\cdot ⟨0, 1⟩ &=  \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 1⟩ \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1834/Source/Images/7448afe0b58173006462d2b229836749.png)" 13194139535661 false] [17592186119502 1145 "Since we are given both the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ and the derivative $\\mathbf T'(t)$ of the unit tangent, we will use the following formula to compute the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\mathbf \\kappa(t)} = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|}{\\left\\| \\mathbf T'(t) \\right\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ $\\mathbf r'(0),$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(0)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨1,t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨1,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate $\\mathbf T'(0)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(0) & =⟨-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{(1+{0}^{2})^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(1+{0}^{2})^{3}}}⟩=⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=⟨0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & = \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩+1\\cdot ⟨0,1⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨0,1⟩ & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/assets/cc9e1eaf1dc8655565a94b7ef0146dc2bb458cc7376828b19352acc01ddbeaad.png)" 13194139535661 true] [17592186119503 1100 "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=0$ for the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle e^{ t}, \\: e^{- t} \\right\\rangle.$" 13194139535661 true] [17592186119503 1100 "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=0$ for the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle e^{t}, \\: e^{-t} \\right\\rangle$." 13194139535661 false] [17592186119503 1145 "Since we are given only the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\kappa(t)} = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}{\\left\\|\\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\right\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$, $\\mathbf r'(0)$, and $\\|\\mathbf r'(0)\\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(e^{t}), \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{-t})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨e^{t},-e^{-t}⟩ \\\\\nr^{′}(0) &= ⟨e^{0},-e^{-0}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\\n‖r^{′}(0)‖ &= \\sqrt{(1)^{2} + (-1)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate $\\mathbf r''(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{″}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(e^{t}), \\frac{d}{dt}(-e^{-t})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨e^{t}, - (-e^{-t})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨e^{t}, e^{-t}⟩ \\\\\nr^{″}(0) &= ⟨e^{0}, e^{-0}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross product by considering $\\mathbf r'(0)$ and $\\mathbf r''(0)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(0) \\cdot r^{″}(0) &= ⟨1,-1, 0⟩ \\cdot ⟨1, 1, 0⟩ \\\\\n&= i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}0 \\\\\n&= 1 \\mid 1\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ = 1\\end{vmatrix}- 1 \\\\\n&= 1 \\mid 1k \\\\\n&= 2k \\\\\n‖r^{′}(0) \\cdot r^{″}(0)‖ &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(0) &= (‖r^{′}(0)‖^{3})/(‖r^{′}(0) \\cdot r^{″}(0)‖) = ((\\sqrt{2})^{3})/(2) = \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535661 false] [17592186119503 1145 "Since we are given only the curve $\\mathbf r(t),$ we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\kappa(t)} = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}{\\left\\|\\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\right\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t),$ $\\mathbf r'(0),$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(0)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,-{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨{e}^{0},\\,-{e}^{-0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate $\\mathbf r''(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,-(-{e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨{e}^{0},\\,{e}^{-0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross product by considering $\\mathbf r'(0)$ and $\\mathbf r''(0)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & \\,-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\,-1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}=\\frac{{(\\sqrt{2})}^{3}}{\\,2\\,}=\\sqrt{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535661 true] [17592186119682 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359729 13194139535661 true] [17592186119682 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359736 13194139535661 true] [17592186119682 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359743 13194139535661 true] [17592186119682 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359785 13194139535661 true] [17592186119682 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359792 13194139535661 true] [17592186119683 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359764 13194139535661 true] [17592186119683 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359771 13194139535661 true] [17592186119683 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359799 13194139535661 true] [17592186119683 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359806 13194139535661 true] [17592186119683 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359813 13194139535661 true] [17592186119684 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359750 13194139535661 true] [17592186119684 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359757 13194139535661 true] [17592186119684 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359778 13194139535661 true] [17592186119684 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359820 13194139535661 true] [17592186120311 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535661 true] [17592186120311 1100 "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t = π$ for the curve $r(t) = ⟨e^{\\sin t}, e^{\\cos t}⟩$." 13194139535661 false] [17592186120311 1100 "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\pi $ for the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t},\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}⟩.$" 13194139535661 true] [17592186120311 1145 "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(\\pi ),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{0},\\,-{e}^{-1}\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+0}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{cos}^{2}⁡t,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{sin}^{2}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨-{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\pi ,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0+1,{e}^{-1}+0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\frac{1}{e}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ and ${r}^{″}(\\pi )$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{e},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1/e & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1/e\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{e}\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖ & =\\frac{1}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi ){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{