13194139535661 true] [17592186359778 1113 #uuid "df705f9d-a2f0-57c1-9293-5cf4d9e544fe" 13194139535661 true] [17592186359778 1145 "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and ${r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨0,\\,-2,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the radius of the osculating circle at  $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}=\\frac{{2}^{3}}{\\,2\\,}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535661 true] [17592186359779 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359780 13194139535661 true] [17592186359779 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359781 13194139535661 true] [17592186359779 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359782 13194139535661 true] [17592186359779 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359783 13194139535661 true] [17592186359779 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359784 13194139535661 true] [17592186359779 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535661 true] [17592186359779 1041 "selection" 13194139535661 true] [17592186359779 1104 #edb/ref 17592186359783 13194139535661 true] [17592186359779 1110 #uuid "e758c140-5a89-5d34-92b7-14516efe1051" 13194139535661 true] [17592186359780 1034 "8" 13194139535661 true] [17592186359780 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535661 true] [17592186359780 1111 #uuid "ae1f7d0f-ef9d-56c7-b8ac-41f49fe36560" 13194139535661 true] [17592186359781 1034 "\\frac{4}{3}" 13194139535661 true] [17592186359781 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535661 true] [17592186359781 1111 #uuid "fc760af9-2de1-5910-8c48-81c477723569" 13194139535661 true] [17592186359782 1034 "1" 13194139535661 true] [17592186359782 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535661 true] [17592186359782 1111 #uuid "682a9c74-e02d-56b0-ab53-052222cefc3c" 13194139535661 true] [17592186359783 1034 "4" 13194139535661 true] [17592186359783 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535661 true] [17592186359783 1111 #uuid "a94e181c-ca3b-59f1-be43-075fd2a4c7f5" 13194139535661 true] [17592186359784 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139535661 true] [17592186359784 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535661 true] [17592186359784 1111 #uuid "d87d4601-f5f1-5b7d-b85d-69971d250dbd" 13194139535661 true] [17592186359785 1017 #edb/ref 17592186359786 13194139535661 true] [17592186359785 1089 "q-64732" 13194139535661 true] [17592186359785 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535661 true] [17592186359785 1100 "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that  its  unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\,k\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535661 true] [17592186359785 1113 #uuid "1a75f579-8ee0-5607-99e3-d5f80c6111fb" 13194139535661 true] [17592186359785 1145 "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature: \n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})k \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i+\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j] \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2}{9})}=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$" 13194139535661 true] [17592186359786 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359787 13194139535661 true] [17592186359786 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359788 13194139535661 true] [17592186359786 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359789 13